<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
{#- A paused claim has had its answer taken down, so "Claim checked" would be false in the one
    place a reader reads before opening the page. Backlog call 28. -#}
<title>{% if claim and paused %}Claim check paused: {{ claim }} | {{ site.name }}{% elif claim %}Claim checked: {{ claim }} | {{ site.name }}{% elif title %}{{ title }} | {{ site.name }}{% else %}{{ site.name }}{% endif %}</title>
{#- `description` is for a page whose meta description should not be its visible strapline. The
    404 page is the only one today: it has no strapline, so it fell through to the site's own and
    was the one duplicated description among the seventeen pages. #}
{#- `short_answer` is skipped on a paused claim for the same reason the page no longer prints it:
    a description tag is published text, and the pause took that answer down. -#}
<meta name="description" content="{% if claim and paused %}This claim check is paused pending re-review. The page says what the claim was and why the check came down.{% else %}{{ description or short_answer or strapline or site.strapline }}{% endif %}">
{#- The two --paper values from content/assets/style.css, which is where the palette lives. They
    cannot be read from CSS by markup, so this is the one place a colour is written twice, and it
    is only the page background: a browser that tints its own chrome otherwise picks white against
    the dark scheme. #}
<meta name="theme-color" content="#fdfdfc" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#16171a" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
{#- Open Graph. A claim page's `og:title` is the CORRECTION and never the claim: a share card is
    where a page travels furthest from its own context, and one leading in bold with the bare false
    sentence is the same defect as an unqualified `h1` with more reach. The claim is still there,
    in the description, labelled as checked. Everywhere else the title is the page's own.

    There is no `og:image`, and that is a decision rather than an omission: the foundation document
    at 8.5.4 refuses a share image, on the reasoning that a claim card gets screenshotted stripped
    of its context by both sides, so the period, source and date live inside the card's own visual
    boundary instead. Platforms fall back to a text card, which is what `twitter:card` names below.
    Without that one, X decides for itself whether to draw a card at all. #}
<meta property="og:type" content="{% if claim %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.name }}">
<meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
{#- A paused claim cannot lead with its correction, because the pause is exactly what took that
    correction down. It leads with the pause instead. What it must never do is fall through to
    the bare claim, which is the defect this whole block exists to prevent. -#}
<meta property="og:title" content="{% if claim and paused %}This claim check is paused pending re-review{% elif claim %}{{ short_answer | truncate(100) }}{% elif title %}{{ title }}{% else %}{{ site.name }}{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{% if claim and paused %}Paused: {{ claim }}{% elif claim %}Claim checked: {{ claim }}{% else %}{{ description or strapline or site.strapline }}{% endif %}">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
{#- Structured data, on the two pages that make these claims to a reader already: the home page
    states who publishes this, and /sources-and-method/ describes the data layer and links every
    file of it. Built in lib/structured-data.mjs, which says what is deliberately absent from it
    and why. Every other page renders nothing here. -#}
{% structuredData %}
<link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css">
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to main content</a>

{# The pre-launch notice was removed here, with the launch. It existed because the site was
   reachable by anyone holding the URL while `robots.txt` refused every crawler, so a reader
   could arrive at a page nothing had announced; the notice was what did the work in that gap.
   Rewriting `robots.txt` under call 26 closes the gap, and a banner saying the site is not
   launched yet, served from the commit that launches it, would be false on every page. It goes
   at launch was decided on 27 July 2026 (PR #54) and is stated in README.md and in
   docs/BACKLOG.md under the review's closing steps. Of the two links it carried, /about/ and
   /sources-and-method/, both are in the navigation on every built page, counted against the
   build rather than assumed. This sentence said "all 23 built pages" until 11 August 2026,
   written when the build made 23 and left behind when it made more; a count of this
   repository in a comment rots exactly like one in a document. The #updates FRAGMENT is not: this banner was the only thing on
   the site linking straight to the update cadence, and after it the page is one click away in
   the navigation and the fragment is not linked from anywhere. That is a reader-facing wording
   question and it is the owner's, so nothing is invented here to replace it. #}

<header class="site-header">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="brand"><a href="/">{{ site.name }}</a></p>
    <nav aria-label="Main">
      {#- A disclosure rather than a flat list. The nine labels need about 980px of line length,
          so at 320px they wrapped to five 50px rows and put every page's h1 below the fold.
          `details` carries this with no client-side JavaScript; above 40em the summary is hidden
          and the list is revealed, so wide screens keep the row they have always had. The
          stylesheet's nav block says how, and what happens where the reveal is unsupported. #}
      <details class="nav-menu">
        <summary>Menu</summary>
        <ul>
          {%- for item in site.nav %}
          <li><a href="{{ item.href }}"{% if page.url == item.href %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a></li>
          {%- endfor %}
        </ul>
      </details>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="main" class="wrap">
  {% if title and not suppressPageHeader %}
  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
    {% if strapline %}<p class="strapline">{{ strapline }}</p>{% endif %}
  </header>
  {% endif %}

  {{ content | safe }}

  {#- "This page was last reviewed" rather than "Last reviewed", because the pre-launch notice
      above states the date of the site-wide pre-publication review and the owner read the two
      as a contradiction. They are different facts and now say which they are. The second
      sentence is stated only where the page has a figure to be true about: it rendered under
      /about/, /common-claims/, /style-guide/ and the 404, none of which prints one. -#}
  {% if last_reviewed %}
  <p class="reviewed">This page was last reviewed on {{ last_reviewed | longDate }}.
  {%- if content | carriesAFigure %} Its figures were the latest published at that date, not a
  live count.{% endif %}</p>
  {% endif %}
</main>

<footer class="site-footer">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>{{ site.strapline }}</p>
    <p>{{ meta.footerNote }}</p>
    <p>Contains public sector information licensed under the
      <a href="https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/">Open Government Licence v3.0</a>.
      <a href="https://github.com/LegendT/UK-Migration-Explorer">Source and data on GitHub</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Found an error?</strong> Tell us which figure and what you think it should be:
      <a href="https://github.com/LegendT/UK-Migration-Explorer/issues/new">open an issue</a>.
      Every change to a published figure is recorded in the
      <a href="https://github.com/LegendT/UK-Migration-Explorer/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">public changelog</a>.</p>
  </div>
</footer>
</body>
</html>
